I writes:
>.
>a problem with object polymorph (nh3.0h6) is that with patience and the
>ability to collect a small stash of garbage you can generate
>ever-increasing amounts of magic. 

	Yes, but there are only so many things that are useful to
	the character.  Using a wand of polymorph is a different
	playing style than some, but is no 'easier.'  I find it
	more entertaining to simply waltz down and slaughter the
	nasties in the castle to get the wand of wishing.  By that
	time I have problems thinking of things I can use.  Sure,
	I may be missing an amulet of life protection or I'm still 
	bashing monsters with an iron ball, but... 

	If someone wants to sit on level 1 with a mound of junk and
	keep zapping wands instead--more power to them!

>if you are so inclined, you can
>generate (sometimes indirectly) everything that the game has to offer
>and you only need a small stash (i.e. 20-30 choice items) to start with!
>that is a flaw. object polymorph could be more problematic ...

	Ok, how about this.  Any item should be able to become any
	other item, and only a small number of objects in the pile
	is to be affected.  That way zapping at 20 wands of digging
	won from minotaurs in no way guarantees 1/5 chance of getting 
	a wand of wishing.

>>	[monsters find it easy to hit me with thrown weapons when
>>	 I'm invisible]
>thrown attacks miss as well as hit when your invisible. sometimes
>this goes unmentioned but you may see the occasion weapon fly past or
>land beside you. in any event, invisibility can't be too powerful if
>there are complaints that you often get hit when your invisible ...

	Try being invisible in a room where a dwarf has picked up
	about 60 rocks.  Every move he's gonna throw one right at
	you, while moseying around in a circle.

	If he can see you well enough to throw rocks and hit, he
	should be able to walk right up and introduce himself.

>hmmmm, how about this: instead of the ring of invisibility making you
>invisible it could toggle your invisible status. thus when you're
>permanently invisible you would have a means of controlling your
>invisibility ...

	I'm all for that.  Or having a ring of aggravate monster make
	your presense known, if you know what I mean.  That way when
	a player makes the mistake of assuming wands of make invisible
	wear off, he/she can still get monsters line up and go at each
	other using the ol' ring of conflict.

	In a different vein, what the bloody blazes does a fricking 
	luckstone do?  I got one and must have sacrificed two 
	bags-o-tricks full of Yetis, Giants and what not by now without
	so much as a yoo-hoo from the Big Dude Upstairs.  Bug or feature?

Spoiler on why a pick-axe is your best friend:


	You can hit yourself with it.  Now you might be saying "what
	a stupid thing to do!"  But if you are lugging around a `blesed
	athame of Elbereth carving', you can reduce yourself to 1 hit
	point very easily and relatively safely every 300 turns.  Gods
	will grant you the golden glow as well as famine relief, BTW.
	So with a ring of boredom resistance, 2000 hit point 3rd level
	characters are quite possible.

	Now compare THAT with a truckload of wands of wishing for game
	spoilage effects.


